  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1233 AM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nModels and ensembles remain in good agreement through Monday,\nwith differences remaining for mid to late next week with the\ndevelopment of an upper high across the west.\n\nSaturday through Monday...the upper pattern remains stable\nthrough Sunday with southwesterly flow and embedded energy\nlifting across Colorado. The threat for thunderstorms with \nheavy rainfall will remain across Colorado Saturday and Sunday, \nbefore the upper high to the southeast begins to build westward.\nPWAT values remain high, in excess of an inch, through Sunday, \ndrying slightly heading into Monday. Multiple waves will impact \nsouthern Colorado. Low level flow will remain southeasterly, \nkeeping moisture across the region, with marginal instability \naround 1000 j/kg each day and weak 0-6km shear. Thunderstorms \nlook to initially develop across the higher terrain during the \nafternoon hours, then spread northeast during the evening and \novernight hours. Flash flooding remains a concern on area burn \nscars, especially Saturday and Sunday. While we do start to dry \nout Monday, burn scar flash flooding threat remains elevated. \nOverall, temperatures continue to look pleasant, with 70s and \n80s across the Plains. \n\nTuesday through Friday...lots of spread in the model guidance\nand ensembles for mid to late week. The ECMWF and Canadian are\nsimilar, with the ECMWF developing a strong upper high centered\nover Colorado. The Canadian isn't quite as strong as the ECMWF,\nand places the high west over the Great Basin. The GFS is\ndeveloping an upper ridge along the west coast, with broad\nnorthwesterly flow across Colorado. The NBM continues to trend\nmore with the ECMWF and Canadian, with drier and warmer\nconditions for mid to late week. High temperatures look to trend\nwarmer, with highs back into the 90s across the Plains. PWAT\nvalues also dry out significantly. This will help limit shower\nand thunderstorm development, and what does develop will likely\nhave lower rain rates and limited flash flood risk.",
